{"name":"Gold Vein","hex":"#d6b956","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"},{"term":"vein","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A blood vessel that transports blood from the capillaries back to the heart.","The entrails of a shrimp."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To mark with veins or a vein-like pattern."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vein"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vein (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a vein is a distinct sheetlike body of crystallized minerals within a rock. Veins form when mineral constituents carried by an aqueous solution within the rock mass are deposited through precipitation. The hydraulic flow involved is usually due to hydrothermal circulation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Rock_with_white_veins_at_Imperia_in_Italy.jpg/330px-Rock_with_white_veins_at_Imperia_in_Italy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vein_(geology)","wikidata":"Q952465"},{"title":"Vein","blocks":[{"text":"A vein is a blood vessel in the circulatory system of humans and most other animals that carries blood towards the heart. Most veins carry deoxygenated blood from the tissues back to the heart; exceptions are those of the pulmonary and fetal circulations which carry oxygenated blood to the heart. In the systemic circulation, arteries carry oxygenated blood away from the heart, and veins return deoxygenated blood to the heart, in the deep veins."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Vein_%28retouched%29.svg/330px-Vein_%28retouched%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vein","wikidata":"Q9609"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Quartz_vein_cape_jervis.jpg/500px-Quartz_vein_cape_jervis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Quartz_vein_cape_jervis.jpg/1280px-Quartz_vein_cape_jervis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Quartz_vein_cape_jervis.jpg/500px-Quartz_vein_cape_jervis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A quartz vein, prominent from the surrounding weathered rock at Cape Jervis, South Australia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AQuartz_vein_cape_jervis.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.